-<![CDATA[
+    <help format="markdown"><![CDATA[
+**GotohScan** is a search tool that finds shorter sequences (usually genes) in
+large database sequences (chromosomes, genomes, ...) by computing all
+semi-global alignments. Thus, the query sequence is never truncated or split
+into subsequences, but always mapped to the database over its complete length.
+The alignment is computed via the Gotoh-alignment algorithm using affine gap
+costs.
+
+-----
+
+# Inputs
+
+- **Input Database** — the (typically large) sequences to search in, FASTA format.
+- **Input Query** — the shorter sequence(s) to locate, FASTA format. The query
+  is never truncated or split, and is always aligned over its complete length.
+- **Database is split into subsequences of size** — the database is processed
+  in chunks of this many nucleotides (default: 10000). This only affects
+  memory usage and progress reporting, not which hits are found.
+- **E-value** — report only hits with an E-value below this threshold. Should
+  be smaller than 10 (default: 1e-3).
+- **Percent identity of aligned sequences** — report only hits with at least
+  this percent identity, in the range [0.0, 100.00].
+- **Print score distribution data for each query to a file** — writes the
+  score distribution per query (xmgrace `.agr` format) next to the result.
+- **Output Format** — see below.
 
-**GotohScan** is a search tool that finds shorter sequences
-(usually genes) in large database sequences (chromosomes, genomes, ..)
-by computing all semi-global alignments.  Thus, the query sequence is
-never truncated or split into subsequences, but always mapped to the
-database over its complete length. The alignment is computed via the
-Gotoh-alignment algorithm using affine gap costs.
+# Output formats
 
+| Value | Format |
+| ----- | ------ |
+| 0 | BLAST tabular output |
+| 1 | BLAST tabular output + aligned sequences |
+| 2 | FASTA — hit sequence only, without gaps |
+| 3 | MAF — note: header is truncated to 30 characters |
+| 4 | BED + aligned sequences |
+| 5 | GFF + aligned sequences |
 ]]></help>
